          body:
            "The older teaching sorted dizziness by the type of sensation: spinning [[vertigo|vertigo]] for the ear, [[presyncope|presyncope]] for the heart, [[disequilibrium|disequilibrium]] for the legs. Patients describe the same problem in different words from one telling to the next, so this sorting is unreliable, and it sends dangerous causes down the wrong path.\n\nThe [[titrate-approach|TiTrATE]] approach (timing, triggers, and a targeted examination) asks instead:\n\n1. Is it continuous, or does it come in episodes?\n2. If episodic, is each episode triggered (by a head movement or standing up) or spontaneous?\n\nThat gives three groups that matter in the emergency department:\n\n- Triggered episodes: brief spells set off by a movement. Common cause: [[bppv|benign paroxysmal positional vertigo]] (BPPV). Also orthostatic hypotension (on standing, not on lying down or rolling over). Dangerous mimic: a posterior fossa lesion that causes positional nystagmus.\n- Spontaneous episodes: spells that come on their own, lasting minutes to hours. Common causes: [[vestibular-migraine|vestibular migraine]] and [[meniere-disease|Meniere disease]]. Dangerous cause: posterior circulation [[transient-ischemic-attack|TIA]].\n- Continuous vertigo for a day or more: the [[acute-vestibular-syndrome|acute vestibular syndrome]]. Common cause: [[vestibular-neuritis|vestibular neuritis]]. Dangerous cause: posterior circulation stroke.\n\nThe GRACE-3 emergency department guideline is built around these same groups.",
