          body:
            "BPPV is the most common cause of triggered episodes. Loose otoconia (calcium carbonate crystals) drift into a semicircular canal, usually the posterior canal. Moving the head in that canal's plane, such as lying down, rolling over in bed, or looking up, makes the debris move and causes brief, intense spinning. Between episodes the examination is normal.\n\nThe [[dix-hallpike-test|Dix-Hallpike test]] confirms posterior canal BPPV. With the head turned 45 degrees toward one ear, the patient is brought quickly from sitting to lying with the head hanging slightly below the couch. A positive test reproduces vertigo with upbeating, torsional nystagmus toward the lower ear, starting after a short delay and fading, usually within a minute. Treat it at the bedside with a [[canalith-repositioning|canalith repositioning maneuver]] (the Epley maneuver), which moves the debris back out of the canal. The 2017 guideline advises against routine imaging and vestibular testing when the diagnosis is typical, against routine vestibular suppressant drugs, and against postural restrictions after the maneuver.\n\nPositional nystagmus that does not fit (purely downbeating, no delay, does not fade, or vomiting out of proportion to the vertigo), or any other neurological sign, raises a central cause and needs imaging.\n\nSpontaneous episodes:\n\n- Vestibular migraine: recurrent vertigo with migraine features (headache, photophobia, visual aura) in at least some episodes, usually with a past history of migraine.\n- Meniere disease: episodes lasting hours with fluctuating hearing loss, tinnitus, and fullness in the same ear.\n- Posterior circulation TIA: brief spontaneous vertigo, sometimes with no other symptom, in a patient with vascular risk factors. New episodes in an older adult deserve urgent vascular imaging, as GRACE-3 advises when TIA is a concern.",
