          body:
            "In the acute vestibular syndrome the question is vestibular neuritis versus a stroke in the cerebellum or brainstem. Many of these strokes cause no limb weakness and no speech problem, so a normal general neurological examination does not exclude them.\n\nYou met the [[hints-exam|HINTS]] examination in the foundations module: an abnormal [[head-impulse-test|head impulse test]], unidirectional horizontal [[nystagmus|nystagmus]], and no [[skew-deviation|skew deviation]] fit neuritis; any one central finding points to stroke. Add two more checks:\n\n- Hearing. New hearing loss on the side of the vertigo does not make it benign. The anterior inferior cerebellar artery supplies the inner ear as well as the pons and cerebellum, so its occlusion causes vertigo with hearing loss. GRACE-3 recommends a quick bedside check such as finger rub.\n- Standing and walking. Most patients with neuritis can stand and walk, unsteadily. Being unable to sit or stand without support suggests a central cause, and GRACE-3 uses the severity of gait unsteadiness especially when there is no nystagmus.\n\nHINTS is meant for patients with continuous vertigo and nystagmus, and it is accurate in trained hands. GRACE-3 notes that it performs poorly when used in the wrong patients or by untrained examiners.\n\nImaging is the trap. CT is insensitive for posterior fossa ischemic stroke, so GRACE-3 recommends against using it to exclude stroke here. Even MRI with [[diffusion-weighted-imaging|diffusion-weighted imaging]] misses some small brainstem strokes in the first 48 hours: in the original HINTS study the bedside exam was more sensitive than early MRI. A central bedside pattern means managing the patient as a stroke whatever the early scan shows, with MRI as the confirmatory test.",
